-Note on allowed operations
---------------------------
-The framework's built-in 1-D convolution primitives are out of scope for this task
-— the operation is built explicitly. The current implementation first materializes
-a left-padded copy of the input, then accumulates the ``K`` taps one at a time
-(each tap a separate full-tensor read/modify/write that round-trips the whole
-``(B, C, L)`` intermediate through global memory), and finally runs the activation
-as one more pass. It is correct but leaves a lot of GPU memory bandwidth and
-kernel-launch overhead on the table.
+Implementation note
+-------------------
+The operation is built explicitly as a single fused Triton kernel: each program
+covers a (ROWS x BLOCK_L) tile of the flattened (batch*row, length) layout, loads
+the shifted taps (masking the left zero-pad region instead of materializing a
+padded copy), accumulates all ``K`` taps plus the bias in float32 registers,
+applies the gating activation in-register, and writes the output once. One kernel
+launch, one read of ``x``, one write of ``y``. For 16-bit dtypes with an even,
+stride-compatible length axis, a packed variant reinterprets the length axis as
+int32 element pairs so every tap is assembled from wide aligned 32-bit loads
+instead of narrow 16-bit ones. Compiled kernels are cached and re-launched through
+the low-level driver entry point to keep per-call host overhead minimal.
